INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "What are the wider supervisory implications of the Wirecard case?", "source_url": "https://safe-frankfurt.de/fileadmin/user_upload/editor_common/Policy_Center/SAFE_Policy_White_Paper_74.pdf", "text": "SRC-002, European Parliament ECON-requested policy briefing, reports: all five lines of defence failed in one way or another—Wirecard internal control system, supervisory board, external audit, oversight bodies for financial reporting/auditing, and market supervisor BaFin. It says signals suggested fraudulent behaviour years before revelation. Timeline: starting 2015 allegations of accounting inconsistencies, especially Financial Times reports based partly on whistleblower information about Singapore; 2016 Zatarra Report accused Wirecard of money laundering and fraud; January 2019 after another FT report BaFin requested a FREP investigation, prohibited increases in net short positions for two months when short selling peaked, and filed a criminal complaint against short sellers and the journalist; FT continued reporting allegedly inflated sales/profits backed by internal documents; KPMG hired October 2019 for forensic investigation; report delayed until April 2020 and stated KPMG was unable to verify existence of €1 billion in foreign bank accounts; BaFin filed criminal complaint against Wirecard in June 2020; days later Wirecard filed for bankruptcy acknowledging €1.9 billion in missing financial assets. The briefing attributes the slow detection to a mix of perpetrator moral failures and institutional deficiencies, and identifies blocked information flows, weaknesses in external audits/internal oversight/market forces, impaired enforcement of financial disclosure, and an overarching/limited market-supervisor mandate. The briefing is an assessment/policy analysis, not a criminal judgment."}
